# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Tool write-ups (description field) for Class 6 Ch4 'Data Handling and Presentation'. Keyed by tool media id."""
DESCRIPTIONS = {
"M1.S4.T4.C11.2D1": (
"The student steps into Shri Nilesh's class on the morning he asks for sweet choices. Across the top runs a row of sweet names (jalebi, gulab jamun, gujiya, barfi, rasgulla), and under each name is an empty tally row with a Tally button, a total box and, at the end, a most-chosen marker. "
"A speech bubble appears with one child's answer, such as 'Gulab jamun, please', and the next bubble comes only after that answer has been tallied. "
"A narrow list of the written answers grows beside the rows, so the student can see how long it gets compared with the neat tally rows.\n\n"
"Before the stream starts, the student predicts which sweet will be chosen most. Then the student taps the Tally button under the sweet named in each bubble. Every tap draws one upright stroke. "
"For the fifth answer the student lays the stroke across the previous four. If it is dropped beside the four instead, it snaps across them into a bundle of five and a short note says that the fifth stroke goes across the four. "
"To read a finished row the student counts the bundles in fives and then the loose strokes, and types the total into the total box.\n\n"
"The six cards begin with a stream of 12 answers over three sweets, tallied and totalled (for example 5, 4 and 3). Next come ready-made rows to read: one bundle and 3 strokes (8), then two bundles and 2 strokes (12). "
"A stream of 20 answers over the five sweets asks which sweet has the greatest frequency. A finished table of jalebi 6, gulab jamun 9, gujiya 13, barfi 3 and rasgulla 7 asks the student to check that the totals add up to the 38 answers. "
"The last card asks which sweet has the least frequency and by how much it is below the most (barfi with 3, which is 10 fewer than gujiya with 13).\n\n"
"A wrong total lights up the bundles one by one with a count in fives, and then the loose strokes. A solved card turns into a frequency table with the tally and the total side by side, and a note beside it says that the table tells how many children chose each sweet but not which child chose which. "
"Hints go in order: what does one stroke stand for? Then count the crossed bundles in fives, then the loose strokes. Then add 5 for each bundle and 1 for each loose stroke. Last, the bundles are lit and counted, the total is shown, and a similar card follows. "
"Through doing, the student learns that one stroke stands for one answer, that bundles of five make a long row quick and safe to read, and that the total of a row is its frequency."
),

"M2.S8.T8.C24.2D1": (
"A pictograph fills the screen. Each category has its own row of symbols and half symbols standing on a common baseline, and above the picture sits a key box that reads 'one symbol stands for ...'. "
"The key box can be switched between 1, 2, 5 and 10. Below the picture are a question strip, an answer box and a Reveal button.\n\n"
"The student must read the key before anything else. Only then does the student tap a row, and its symbols light up one by one as they are counted. The student works out the count and types it, or picks the row the question asks for. "
"Reveal then shows the count under the tapped row, so the student can check the working. A half symbol is drawn clearly as half the width of a full symbol, so it looks like half of one symbol and is never mistaken for a whole one. "
"If the student counts the symbols without reading the key, the key box flashes and the count is not accepted until the key has been used.\n\n"
"The six cards cover the cases the chapter reads. With the key at 1 student, the travel-to-school picture asks which row is longest (the school bus, with 11 students). With the key at 10 children, a row of 5 symbols is 50, and a row of 2 symbols and a half symbol is 25. "
"With the key at 2 books, 3 symbols and a half symbol make 7. An empty row, such as Thursday in the library picture, is 0 and means nobody borrowed a book. "
"On the last card the same row of symbols is shown first under key 5 and then under key 10, and the student sees that the count doubles with the bigger key even though the picture has not changed.\n\n"
"A solved card shows the working as a short sum, such as 2 times 10 plus 5 equals 25. "
"Hints move in order: what does one symbol stand for? Then count the full symbols and see whether there is a half symbol. Then multiply the full symbols by the key and add half the key for a half symbol. Last, the sum is shown step by step and a similar card follows. "
"Through doing, the student learns that a pictograph cannot be read by counting pictures alone: the key comes first, a half symbol is half the key, an empty row is 0, and the same picture means different numbers under different keys."
),

"M2.S10.T10.C31.2D1": (
"The student begins with a table of categories and numbers on one side and, beside it, an empty pictograph with one blank row for each category. Above the rows is a key picker (1, 2, 5, 10 and 100), and a tray holds full symbols and half symbols to drop into the rows. "
"Under the rows is a check strip that multiplies the symbols in each row by the key and puts the result next to the table number.\n\n"
"The student first chooses a key, then divides each table number by it in the head and drops that many symbols into the row. When the number leaves exactly half a key over, the student adds a half symbol. "
"After pressing Check, every row is compared with the table. A key that leaves a remainder which is not half a key shows the leftover and asks for a better key. "
"A key that gives too many symbols in a row, such as the key 1 on rows of 20 to 45, fills the row and shows a crowded warning that suggests a bigger key.\n\n"
"The six cards start with Mudhol dogs in six villages, 18, 36, 12, 48, 18 and 24, with the card's own key of 6 (3, 6, 2, 8, 3 and 4 symbols). Next, kites sold to three shopkeepers, 250, 300 and 100, use the key 100 (2 and a half, 3 and 1 symbols). "
"Then 25 students use the key 10 (2 symbols and a half symbol). A fourth card asks why the key 10 is a poor choice for 33 students: the student drops 3 symbols and a half symbol and the check strip shows 35, with 3 students left that no half symbol can show. "
"On the fifth card the student picks a good key for 20, 30 and 45. Key 5 gives 4, 6 and 9 symbols, and key 10 gives 2, 3 and 4 and a half. Key 15 leaves 5 over on 20, which is not half of 15, so it is rejected. The last card draws 7 books with the key 2 (3 symbols and a half symbol).\n\n"
"A solved card shows the symbols times the key beside the table number. "
"Hints go in order: which key divides most of the numbers? Then the frequency divided by the key gives the symbols, and half a key is a half symbol. Then divide each number by the chosen key and place that many symbols. Last, the symbols are placed and checked, and a similar card follows. "
"Through doing, the student learns to choose a key to suit the numbers, to divide to find the symbols, and to check by multiplying back."
),

"M3.S12.T12.C37.2D1": (
"The student studies a bar graph whose bars stand against equally spaced guide lines, with a scale label fixed above it, for example 'Scale: 1 unit = 100 vehicles'. "
"Every step between neighbouring guide lines is the same size and is numbered, so equal steps stand for equal amounts. Below the graph are a question strip and a number entry.\n\n"
"The student reads the scale label first. Then the student taps a bar and the guide lines light up one by one from the base to the top, ending with a tag such as '12 units'. "
"The student turns the units into a value with the scale, and types it, or types a total or a difference when the question asks for one. "
"If the student types the unit length instead of the value, the scale label moves beside the bar to show what a unit stands for. A bar of no height is labelled 0, so an empty class can be seen and not mistaken for a missing bar.\n\n"
"The six cards begin with the graph of students absent in each class, where 1 unit is 1 student and the Class 2 bar of 5 units means 5 students. Next, the traffic graph with horizontal bars and 1 unit equal to 100 vehicles asks for a bar of 12 units (1200 vehicles). "
"Back on the absent-students graph, the student names the longest and the shortest bar (Class 8 and Class 5, whose bar has no height). The student adds two traffic bars read at 1000 and 800 vehicles (1800). "
"On the population graph, the bars for the first and the last decade read 36 crores and 102 crores, and the student subtracts to find the increase (66 crores). The last card keeps the scale at 1 unit equal to 10 crores and asks for a bar of 8 units (80 crores).\n\n"
"A solved card shows units times scale as a short sum, with the bar highlighted. "
"Hints go in order: what does one unit of bar length stand for? Then count the units to the top of the bar. Then multiply the units by the scale. Last, the sum is shown with the bar highlighted and a similar card follows. "
"Through doing, the student learns that the length of a bar is read with its scale before it becomes a number, that totals come from adding readings and differences from subtracting them, and that a bar of no height means 0."
),

"M3.S14.T14.C49.2D1": (
"A drawing board shows a table of categories and numbers on the left. On the right are empty axes: a horizontal line with equal bar slots separated by equal gaps, one slot for each category, and a vertical line standing on a zero mark. "
"Beside the vertical line is a scale picker (1, 2, 4, 5, 10 and 200 for each unit), a Check button and a small note of the largest number in the table.\n\n"
"The student first looks at the largest number and sets a scale that will fit it. Setting the scale writes the values along the vertical axis, for example 0, 200, 400 and so on. "
"Then the student divides each number by the scale to find the height of its bar in units, and drags the top of the bar up to that mark. "
"If the scale is too small, the bar runs off the paper, the overflow is shown, and the tool asks for a bigger scale. A bar that does not rise from zero is rejected with the start of the axis highlighted. All bars keep the same width and the same gaps, so only the heights differ.\n\n"
"The six cards give the scale to use. Samantha's insects 6, 10, 5, 3 and 2 use scale 1. Smriti's runs 80, 50, 10, 100, 90, 0, 90 and 50 use scale 10, giving 8, 5, 1, 10, 9, 0, 9 and 5 units, and the 0 bar has no height. "
"Railway tickets 24, 20, 16 and 28 use scale 4 (6, 5, 4 and 7 units). Students 45, 30, 20, 10 and 15 use scale 5 (9, 6, 4, 2 and 3 units). Imran's family spending of 3000, 3400, 800, 400, 600 and 1200 rupees uses 200 rupees for each unit (15, 17, 4, 2, 3 and 6 units), and the student then names the longest bar (food) and the next (house rent). "
"On the last card the graph is already drawn from the ticket table and one bar does not match its number. The student divides, finds the bar that is wrong (Indore is drawn 4 and a half units tall, but 28 divided by 4 is 7), and drags it to the right height.\n\n"
"A solved card shows each number divided by the scale beside its bar. "
"Hints go in order: what is the largest value, and what scale fits it? Then divide each frequency by the scale to find the bar height. Then divide the number for this bar by the scale and drag it up that many units. Last, the heights are filled in and checked, and a similar card follows. "
"Through doing, the student learns to choose a scale from the largest value, to turn a frequency into a bar height, and to draw equal bars from zero."
),

"M4.S18.T18.C71.2D1": (
"The student plays the part of a fact checker for a poster about the tallest mountain on each continent. On one side is the table: Everest 8848 m, Aconcagua 6962 m, Denali 6194 m, Kilimanjaro 5895 m, Elbrus 5642 m, Vinson Massif 4892 m and Koscuiszko 2228 m. "
"Beside it stand a plain column graph with bars of equal width and a decorated picture of the same mountains drawn as triangles. A ruler line can be dragged over either picture to measure heights and widths, and a doubling check and a vertical or horizontal bar chooser sit below.\n\n"
"The student studies the plain column graph and names the tallest mountain (Everest). Then the student turns to the triangle picture and measures with the ruler. The taller triangles are also wider, but the table gives heights only, so the picture suggests something the data never said. "
"The student marks the picture honest or misleading. If the student calls a picture honest without measuring, the ruler comes up first and the student is asked for a measurement. A misleading picture is then shown next to the equal-width bars for the same table.\n\n"
"In the mountain-range picture Everest looks twice as tall as Elbrus. The student uses the doubling check, enters 5642 times 2 as 11284, and compares it with 8848. Since 8848 is less than 11284, Everest is not twice as tall. "
"Two direction cards follow. For the heights of the tallest persons in each class the student picks vertical bars, and for the lengths of the longest rivers the student picks horizontal bars. The preview turns to match each choice, and a note says that heights go upward and lengths lie along the ground, though the other direction could also be drawn. "
"On the last card, Denali (6194 m) and Kilimanjaro (5895 m) look almost equal on the graph, so the student subtracts to find that Denali is 299 m taller.\n\n"
"A solved card shows the table value, the picture value and the check together. "
"Hints go in order: what does the table say about each value? Then measure the picture with the ruler and compare it with the table. Then double the smaller value and compare it with the larger one. Last, the measurement and the comparison are shown, and a similar card follows. "
"Through doing, the student learns that a picture can look convincing and still mislead, that equal-width bars compare only heights, and that every picture should be checked against the numbers."
),
}
